== demo: 20 concurrent buyers per scenario, 10 items available ==

## 1. Lost update (READ COMMITTED)
   concept: read stock, compute stock - 1 in the app, write it back: concurrent buyers overwrite each other
   20 buyers: 20 told "sold", 0 told "sold out"
   database: 1 recorded as sold for 10 available -> OVERSOLD
   check ok: lost update under READ COMMITTED: more buyers told "sold" than stock went down (oversold)
   stock left: 9 (every buyer read 10 and wrote 9)

## 2. Not needed: REPEATABLE READ catches the lost update
   concept: the second writer of the same row gets 40001 instead of overwriting; retry the transaction and it reads the new stock
   20 buyers: 10 told "sold", 10 told "sold out"
   database: 10 recorded as sold for 10 available -> correct
   check ok: REPEATABLE READ with retries sells exactly 10, no lost update
   aborted with: could not serialize access due to concurrent update
   retries this run: 144
   check ok: REPEATABLE READ aborted the second writers with "concurrent update" (40001)

## 3. Not needed: one atomic statement
   concept: UPDATE ... SET stock = stock - 1 WHERE stock > 0 locks the row and re-checks the condition; READ COMMITTED is enough
   20 buyers: 10 told "sold", 10 told "sold out"
   database: 10 recorded as sold for 10 available -> correct
   check ok: one atomic UPDATE ... WHERE stock > 0 sells exactly 10

## 4. Not needed: a unique constraint
   concept: 20 buyers want seat A1; the check in the app is racy, but UNIQUE (event_id, seat) rejects every duplicate at insert
   20 buyers: 1 told "sold", 19 told "sold out"
   database: 1 recorded as sold for 1 available -> correct
   check ok: UNIQUE (event_id, seat) sells seat A1 exactly once

## 5. Write skew (READ COMMITTED)
   concept: capacity is a rule over many rows: count the tickets, then insert a new one; the ticket rows that would conflict do not exist yet, so all buyers pass the check
   20 buyers: 20 told "sold", 0 told "sold out"
   database: 20 recorded as sold for 10 available -> OVERSOLD
   check ok: write skew happens under READ COMMITTED: oversold

## 6. Write skew (REPEATABLE READ)
   concept: a snapshot makes each transaction's reads stable, but they still do not see each other's inserts: same oversell
   20 buyers: 20 told "sold", 0 told "sold out"
   database: 20 recorded as sold for 10 available -> OVERSOLD
   check ok: write skew happens under REPEATABLE READ too: oversold

## 7. Needed: SERIALIZABLE, with retries
   concept: Postgres tracks read/write dependencies (SSI) and aborts one side of each conflict with 40001; the app retries the whole transaction
   20 buyers: 10 told "sold", 10 told "sold out"
   database: 10 recorded as sold for 10 available -> correct
   check ok: SERIALIZABLE with retries prevents the write skew: exactly 10 sold
   aborted with: could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions
   retries this run: 131
   check ok: SERIALIZABLE aborted one side of each conflict with "read/write dependencies" (40001)

## 8. Alternative: lock the parent row
   concept: SELECT ... FROM events FOR UPDATE turns the many-row rule into a one-row lock; READ COMMITTED, no retries, but buyers queue
   20 buyers: 10 told "sold", 10 told "sold out"
   database: 10 recorded as sold for 10 available -> correct
   check ok: locking the parent row under READ COMMITTED also sells exactly 10
   check ok: the last scenario left exactly 10 tickets for 10 distinct buyers

